  "question": {
    "text": "An electric instrument consists of two units. Each unit must function independently for the instrument to operate. The probability that the first unit functions is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn></math> and that of the second unit is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The instrument is switched on and it fails to operate. If the probability that only the first unit failed and second unit is functioning is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>98</mn><mi>p</mi></math> is equal to",
    "image": null
